 barriers so that we can  get more of our fantastic british  products  into those fast-growing markets so for  example in malaysia  currently they're paying over 100  tariffs on whiskey  if we could get those tariffs reduced it  means more exports more opportunities  for the scotch industry in in scotland  um  these things are always difficult to  assess we will produce a scoping study  when we start the formal negotiations  which is likely to be  later on in the spring but fundamentally  what it is about is lowering barriers to  opportunities for british business  without wanting to you know diminish the  importance of signing up to these um  partnerships or trade deals any  assessment is likely to  pale in comparison to the gdp that we  lost  after leaving the single market after  leaving  the european union isn't it i mean the  obr estimates for example that standard  trade deal like the one that we  negotiated would  reduce our gdp by around four percent  compared to the previous relationship  well i don't i don't accept the premise  of that question because all of the  scenarios the uk economy is growing  and we have secured now a brexit deal  well we've secured a brexit deal that is  tariff-free and quota free so now we've  got the best of both worlds  we can trade freely with the european  union and trade freely with these  fast-growing parts of the world  and if you add up cpt bpp and the united  states  that's 40 of global gdp that is a huge  market for us to go after  you know we're the second largest  services expert we haven't got to deal  with it  we haven't got traded with the united  states yet haven't we well we're working  on that as well safely  those are both in the pipeline but my  point is there is these huge  opportunities  out there and those markets are growing  faster  than the eu market so we need to think  about where do we want britain to be in  2030  where do we want us to be in 2050 when  digital trade is much more important  when briti